  "problem_statement": "*** Problem Statement ***\n\n\nClassification of bi-Iwahori orbits on \\(M_{n \\times m}(F)\\)\n\nSetup\n\nLet \\(F\\) be a non-archimedean local field with ring of integers \\(\\mathcal{O}\\) and uniformizer \\(\\varphi\\). Let \\(n, m \\geq 1\\) be integers. The *Iwahori subgroup* \\(I \\subset \\mathrm{GL}_n(F)\\) is defined as the set of invertible \\(n \\times n\\) matrices \\(g = (g_{ij})\\) such that:\n\\[\n\\mathrm{val}(g_{ij}) \\geq 0 \\;\\; \\text{for } i \\leq j \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\mathrm{val}(g_{ij}) \\geq 1 \\;\\; \\text{for } i > j.\n\\]\nSimilarly, the Iwahori subgroup \\(I' \\subset \\mathrm{GL}_m(F)\\) consists of invertible \\(m \\times m\\) matrices with the same valuation conditions.\n\nThe groups \\(\\mathrm{GL}_n(F)\\) and \\(\\mathrm{GL}_m(F)\\) act on the space of \\(n \\times m\\) matrices \\(M_{n \\times m}(F)\\) by\n\\[\n(g,h) \\cdot M = g M h^{-1}\n\\quad \\text{for } g \\in \\mathrm{GL}_n(F),\\; h \\in \\mathrm{GL}_m(F),\\; M \\in M_{n \\times m}(F).\n\\]\nRestricting to the Iwahori subgroups, we obtain the *bi-Iwahori action* of \\(I \\times I'\\) on \\(M_{n \\times m}(F)\\).\n\nLet \\(E_{ij}\\) denote the elementary matrix with \\(1\\) at position \\((i,j)\\) and \\(0\\) elsewhere.\n\nA *\\(\\mathbb{Z}\\)-colored partial matching* is a subset \\(\\sigma \\subseteq [n] \\times [m] \\times \\mathbb{Z}\\) such that each row index \\(i \\in [n]\\) appears in at most one triple of \\(\\sigma\\), and each column index \\(j \\in [m]\\) appears in at most one triple of \\(\\sigma\\). Equivalently, the pairs \\((i,j)\\) occurring in \\(\\sigma\\) form a partial matching between \\([n]\\) and \\([m]\\).\n\nFor such \\(\\sigma\\), define\n\\[\nE_\\sigma := \\sum_{(i,j,v) \\in \\sigma} \\varphi^v E_{ij}.\n\\]\n(Here \\([n] := \\{1, \\ldots, n\\}\\) and \\([m] := \\{1, \\ldots, m\\}\\).)\n\n**Goal:** Prove that the bi-Iwahori orbits on \\(M_{n \\times m}(F)\\) are classified by \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\)-colored partial matchings. Precisely:\n1. *Surjectivity:* Every matrix in \\(M_{n \\times m}(F)\\) lies in the orbit of \\(E_\\sigma\\) for some \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\)-colored partial matching \\(\\sigma\\).\n2. *Distinctness:* For \\(\\mathbb{Z}\\)-colored partial matchings \\(\\sigma\\) and \\(\\tau\\), the matrices \\(E_\\sigma\\) and \\(E_\\tau\\) lie in the same orbit if and only if \\(\\sigma = \\tau\\).\n\n\n",
